AquaStream360's Network Calibration Tool

Step-by-Step Guide
________________________________________
Welcome to Network Calibration
What Is This Tool?
This is a water network calibration and testing application. It allows you to safely experiment with changes to your water distribution system—pipes, pumps, tanks, valves, and junctions—without affecting your original network model.
It provides a controlled testing environment where you can evaluate multiple scenarios, analyze outcomes, and make informed decisions before applying changes to a production network.
Key Benefits
•	Test multiple scenarios without risking original data
•	Identify optimal component values through systematic simulation
•	Understand how changes affect pressure, flow, and water quality
•	Make data-driven decisions before implementing real-world updates
________________________________________
Part 1: Getting Started
A. Launching the Application
Initial Setup
•	The application automatically loads an example network (Net3) on startup so you can explore features immediately.
•	You may import your own network at any time using the Import New Net button in the top-left corner.
Interface Overview
The interface is divided into two main sections:
•	Left Panel: Controls for defining test scenarios and running simulations
•	Right Panel: Results display, analysis tools, and reporting options
________________________________________
Part 2: The Testing Process (Step by Step)
Step 1: Define What You Want to Test
1.1 Select Element Type
Use the Element Type To Test dropdown to choose the component category you want to analyze:
•	junction
•	pipe
•	tank
•	valve
•	reservoir
•	pump
Example: Select pipe to evaluate pipe-related attributes.
1.2 Choose Which Elements
•	All Elements: Tests every element of the selected type
•	Specific Element: Tests only the selected element
Example: Choose All Elements to test all pipes simultaneously.
1.3 Select the Attribute to Modify
Available attributes depend on the selected element type.
Examples:
•	Pipes: length, diameter, roughness, minor_loss, check_valve
•	Junctions: base_demand, elevation
Example: Select diameter to evaluate different pipe sizes.
________________________________________
Step 2: Define How You Want to Test
2.1 Choose Test Condition
Available test conditions include:
•	In Range: Tests values between two limits
•	In List: Tests specific values
•	Above: Tests values greater than a threshold
•	Below: Tests values less than a threshold
•	Closest to: Finds values closest to a target
•	Is: Used for True/False attributes only
2.2 Enter Test Values
•	In Range
Example formats: 0.1-0.5, 0.1:0.5, or 0.1;0.5
The tool automatically generates 10 evenly spaced values.
•	In List
Example: 0.1, 0.3, 0.5, 0.7
Commas, spaces, or semicolons may be used.
•	Above / Below
Enter a single threshold value.
•	Closest to
Enter a target value.
2.3 Set Proximity Factor (If Applicable)
This option appears for Above, Below, and Closest to conditions.
Purpose: Controls how far apart test values are.
Example with target value = 100 and proximity factor = 0.1:
•	Above: 100, 110, 120, 130, ...
•	Below: 100, 90, 80, 70, ...
•	Closest to: Values within ±10% of 100
________________________________________
Part 3: Running Simulations
Click Run Simulation to begin.
What happens:
•	The tool generates multiple test scenarios
•	Each test value is simulated independently
•	Progress is shown in the progress bar
Important note:
If you change any input after running simulations, the Run Simulation button turns red to indicate that results are outdated and must be regenerated.
