Section 3: Intelligent Automation Features
The Network Testing and Calibration Tool includes advanced automation features designed to streamline your workflow, reduce manual effort, and prevent common errors. These intelligent systems work in the background to make calibration faster, easier, and more reliable.
________________________________________
3.1 What “Automation” Means in This Tool
In this module, automation refers to the system’s ability to:
•	Anticipate your needs based on current selections
•	Perform routine calculations and configurations automatically
•	Prevent invalid operations before they occur
•	Guide you through complex workflows with minimal manual input
________________________________________
3.2 Key Automation Features
A. Smart Default Values
How it works:
•	Automatically suggests target values based on your network data
•	Populates reasonable test ranges based on selected conditions
•	Applies proper measurement units from your network settings
Example: Selecting "Pressure" analysis → system auto-suggests the average pressure value from your simulation results.
User action: Review and adjust suggested values if needed.
________________________________________
B. Dynamic Interface Adaptation
How it works:
•	Shows only relevant options, hiding irrelevant controls
•	Displays new inputs when specific operations require them
•	Updates placeholder text to guide expected input
•	Automatically toggles units based on context
Example: Choosing "In Range" condition → input field shows “Enter range (e.g., 5–10)”
Benefit: Reduces confusion and keeps the interface uncluttered.
________________________________________
C. Intelligent Test Value Generation
How it works:
•	Automatically generates test values for conditions like Above/Below or Closest To
•	Distributes values evenly for range testing
•	Uses your specified gap ratio or target value
Example: Setting “Above 50 with gap ratio 0.1” → system tests: 55, 60, 65… 100
User action: Just set the base value; the tool handles the rest.
________________________________________
D. Context-Aware Validation
How it works:
•	Blocks simulation attempts with missing inputs
•	Highlights incomplete fields in red
•	Disables buttons until prerequisites are met
•	Shows clear error messages for invalid inputs
Benefit: Prevents wasted time on failed simulations.
________________________________________
E. Automated Analysis Configuration
How it works:
•	Sets visualization parameters automatically
•	Generates meaningful chart titles and axis labels
•	Selects appropriate statistical measures
•	Organizes results for easy comparison
Example: Time series analysis → system creates hourly data tables for all test scenarios.
________________________________________
3.3 Leveraging Automation Effectively
Best Practices:
1.	Start with suggested values and refine as needed
2.	Use automated test generation for exploratory analysis
3.	Follow visual cues: red warnings indicate required actions; enabled buttons show available operations
4.	Trust the interface adaptation to focus only on relevant options
When to Override Automation:
•	You have domain-specific knowledge requiring custom values
•	Regulatory standards dictate precise test protocols
•	Historical data suggests different test ranges
•	You need to troubleshoot edge cases
